{{~ if is_random_topic ~}}
FIRST choose a random interesting trivia topic (e.g., history, science, art, literature, sports, movies, geography, technology, culture, etc.). Then create a set of trivia questions in English on that chosen topic.
{{~ else ~}}
Create a set of trivia questions in English on the topic "{{ topic }}".
{{~ end ~}}
{{~ if is_movie_topic }}

🎬 IMPORTANT FOR "MOVIES/CINEMA" TOPIC:
✅ FOCUS ON:
- Specific movies and their plots
- Actors and their famous roles
- Directors and their filmography
- Awards (Oscars, Palme d'Or, etc.)
- Famous movie quotes
- Characters and their names
- Release years of famous films
- Genres and franchises

❌ AVOID TECHNICAL ASPECTS:
- Questions about filmmaking (how movies are made)
- Cinematography and cameras
- Editing and post-production
- Special effects and CGI technology
- Technical equipment
{{~ end }} Follow these requirements:
{{~ if is_random_topic }}
- Generate {{ questions }} questions about your chosen topic.
{{~ else }}
- Generate {{ questions }} questions about the topic "{{ topic }}".
{{~ end ~}}
{{~ if difficulty == "Easy" }}

🎯 EASY LEVEL - SIMPLE AND STRAIGHTFORWARD QUESTIONS:

✅ QUESTION STYLE:
   • Use direct, simple formulations
   • Ask about widely known facts
   • Focus on popular topics, famous people, major events
   • Example: "What is the capital of France?" → Answer: "Paris"
   • Example: "Who wrote 'Romeo and Juliet'?" → Answer: "William Shakespeare"
   • Example: "How many continents are there?" → Answer: "Seven"

✅ CHOOSE TOPICS ABOUT:
   • Basic facts from general education
   • Well-known historical events and figures
   • Popular movies, books, songs
   • Simple geography and nature

❌ AVOID:
   • Complex riddles and metaphors
   • Obscure details and facts
   • Requiring exact dates (except most famous ones)
   • Specialized knowledge

{{~ else if difficulty == "Medium" }}

🎯 MEDIUM LEVEL - BALANCE BETWEEN KNOWLEDGE AND LOGIC:

✅ QUESTION STYLE:
   • Combine direct questions with elements of logic
   • Use moderate metaphors and descriptions
   • Ask about less obvious but not too obscure facts
   • Example: "This Russian poet died in a duel at age 37." → Answer: "Alexander Pushkin"
   • Example: "Which metal is called 'white gold'?" → Answer: "Platinum"
   • Example: "In which country did ballet originate?" → Answer: "Italy"

✅ CHOOSE TOPICS ABOUT:
   • Facts requiring basic erudition
   • Logical connections between events
   • Moderately well-known cultural phenomena
   • Interesting scientific facts

❌ AVOID:
   • Too simple school-level questions
   • Very complex riddles
   • Highly specialized knowledge

{{~ else if difficulty == "Hard" }}

🎯 HARD LEVEL - 'QUIZ SHOW' STYLE - LOGIC AND ASSOCIATIONS:

✅ PREFER questions that can be FIGURED OUT:
   • Use metaphors, allegories, creative descriptions
   • Describe properties, characteristics, functions of the subject
   • Create riddles through associations and logical connections
   • Example: "In a fairy tale by modern writer Eugene Klyuev, the title character grew thinner day by day until dying of complete exhaustion. That was its purpose. Name this character in two words." → Answer: "Tear-off calendar"
   • Example: "This musical instrument got its name from Italian words for 'soft' and 'loud', reflecting its key feature." → Answer: "Pianoforte"
   • Example: "In ancient times this substance was valued its weight in gold and used as currency. Now it's in every kitchen." → Answer: "Salt"

❌ AVOID highly specialized knowledge:
   • DON'T require exact dates (except most famous events)
   • DON'T ask about obscure people's names or technical jargon
   • DON'T create questions requiring professional expertise
   • Bad: "In what year was composer Anton Rubinstein born?" (narrow knowledge)
   • Good: "This Russian composer founded Russia's first conservatory. His surname matches the name of a precious stone." (can be figured out)

{{~ end }}
- Each question should be clear and concise, suitable for a general audience.
- Provide a correct short answer for each question (one word or a few words at most).
- Questions should be interesting and thought-provoking, using logic and associations.
- Avoid ambiguous questions - each should have a single clear correct answer.
- Ensure the questions are varied and cover different aspects of the topic.
{{~ if mature }}
- Mature Content (18+): Adult themes are allowed (erotica, Renaissance art with nudity, mythology with explicit themes, history of sexuality, etc.). Questions should be informative and educational, not vulgar.
{{~ else }}
- Family-Friendly Content: Avoid adult themes. All questions should be suitable for a family audience.
{{~ end }}
- For approximately {{ questions_with_images }} questions, include a DIRECT image URL from RELIABLE public sources.
  IMPORTANT - Use ONLY these verified sources:
  • Unsplash CDN: https://images.unsplash.com/photo-...
  • Wikimedia Commons DIRECT links: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/.../400px-filename.jpg (use 320px, 400px or 640px instead of large sizes!)
  • Pixabay CDN: https://pixabay.com/get/...
  • Pexels CDN: https://images.pexels.com/photos/...
  Do NOT use Wikipedia page links (wiki/File:...) - only direct upload.wikimedia.org links!
  ⚠️ SIZE MATTERS: For Wikimedia use SMALLER sizes (320px, 400px, 640px) - large images (800px+) often fail to load!
  URL MUST end with .jpg, .png, or .webp
  Choose questions where an image is genuinely helpful (landmarks, animals, plants, flags, maps, etc.).
{{~ if archived != empty }}

🚫🚫🚫 CRITICAL - STRICTLY AVOID DUPLICATES! 🚫🚫🚫

These {{ archived.size }} questions have ALREADY been used in recent games.
⛔ ABSOLUTELY FORBIDDEN to repeat them in ANY form:

{{ for q in archived ~}}
{{ q }}
{{ end ~}}

✅ MANDATORY REQUIREMENTS FOR NEW QUESTIONS:

1️⃣ CHECK EACH QUESTION: Ensure it's NOT similar to any question from the list above
2️⃣ AVOID THE OBVIOUS: Don't ask the most well-known, cliché questions about the topic
   ❌ Bad: "Who wrote War and Peace?" - too popular
   ✅ Good: "Which Tolstoy novel begins with a description of Anna Pavlovna Scherer's soirée?"
3️⃣ DIG DEEPER: Ask about lesser-known details, secondary characters, unusual facts
4️⃣ CHANGE THE ANGLE: Instead of "Who wrote it?" ask about plot, characters, dates, influence, criticism
5️⃣ DIVERSITY: Cover different periods, countries, genres, styles
6️⃣ NO REPHRASING: Don't reformulate existing questions - create completely new ones!

⚠️ WARNING: Generating a question similar to an already used one will be considered an error!

{{~ end }}
- Format the output exactly as:
Question: <question text>?
Answer: <answer text>
Image: <image URL> (only if applicable)
(No additional explanations or commentary.)
